<h2>Twenty Commands — Slash Commands in the Order You Use Them: Start, Work, Review, Run</h2>

<p>If Claude Code has felt sluggish lately, there is something to check before blaming the model: the <strong>slash commands</strong> that appear when you type <code>/</code> in the prompt. The same model works quite differently depending on whether you know them. Opus 5.5, for example, defaults to <code>medium</code> effort, the middle of five levels, while the official docs recommend <code>high</code> for work where verification matters, such as fixing a bug. If you don't know that, you keep handing hard tasks to <code>medium</code>.</p>

<p>The sticking points are usually the same. Answers get vague as the conversation grows, you wait on one task in one window, and something reported as done doesn't run. This page takes the 20 commands that address those three problems, splits them into <strong>four groups in the order you use them</strong>, and gives each one a plain explanation and an example you can type as is.</p>

